<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_document" style="font-size:144%;">SoK: Large Language Model-Generated Textual Phishing Campaigns End-to-End Analysis of
<br class="ltx_break">Generation, Characteristics, and Detection</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fengchao Chen</span>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Email:fengchao.chen@monash.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname"><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tingmin Wu</span>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">van.nguyen1@monash.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname"><span id="id5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Van Nguyen</span>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">carsten.rudolph@monash.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname"><span id="id7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Carsten Rudolph<sup id="id7.1" class="ltx_sup">*</sup></span>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tina.wu@data61.csiro.au</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname"><sup id="id9" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">*</span></sup><span id="id10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Faculty of Information Technology</span>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname"><span id="id11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> 
CSIRO Data61 Melbourne Australia</span>
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Phishing is a pervasive form of social engineering in which attackers impersonate trusted entities to steal information or induce harmful actions. Text-based phishing dominates for its low cost, scalability, and concealability, advantages recently amplified by large language models (LLMs) that enable “Phishing-as-a-Service” attacks at scale within minutes. Despite the growing research into LLM-facilitated phishing attacks, consolidated systematic research on the phishing attack life cycle remains scarce. In this work, we present the first systematization of knowledge (SoK) on LLM-generated phishing, offering an end-to-end analysis that spans generation techniques, attack features, and mitigation strategies. We introduce <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generation-Characterization-Defense (GenCharDef)</span>, which systematizes the ways in which LLM-generated phishing differs from traditional phishing across methodologies, security perspectives, data dependencies, and evaluation practices. This framework highlights unique challenges of LLM-driven phishing, providing a coherent foundation for understanding the evolving threat landscape and guiding the design of more resilient defenses.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Phishing is a widespread form of social engineering in which adversaries masquerade as trusted vendors to trick victims into disclosing sensitive data such as authentication credentials <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>. In the first season of 2025 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">90</a>]</cite>, phishing has become a leading vector in cyberattacks, inflicting over $45 billion in global losses alone. While phishing campaigns exploit various channels (e.g., counterfeit webpages <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>, voice calls <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, etc.), the script-based phishing remains the most prevalent transmission method. As reported in Global Phishing Statistics Report <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, approximately 96% of phishing attempts are delivered via email, the primary tool for psychological manipulation across phishing vectors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) possess the capability for producing human-like text aligned with specific instructions, yet the prowess that is weaponized to drive the growth of “Phishing-as-a-Service” operations by adversaries <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>]</cite>. According to KnowBe4’s 2025 Phishing Threat Trends Report <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>]</cite>, 82% of phishing emails incorporate LLMs into phishing campaigns. More alarmingly, a mid-2025 evaluation found that LLM-generated spear phishing emails achieved click-through rates exceeding 30%, outpacing those human-crafted malicious attempts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>. These findings indicate an enhanced, sophisticated, and effective phishing operation weaponized by LLMs at scale <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Thus, it is imperative to systematically understand the full-stack of LLM-weaponized scripted-based phishing diverges from traditional methods to inform the advancement of adaptive countermeasures. In this study, we mainly focus on LLM-driven text-based phishing attacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recent scholarly literature has examined the security vulnerabilities and risks of misuse of LLMs, encompassing various perspectives. Mathew et al.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite> document how prompt injection and jailbreaking coerce LLMs into unsafe outputs, and evaluate existing defensive mechanisms and robustness benchmarks, yet lacking a systematic analysis of phishing-specific generation strategies. Schmitt and Flechais <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>]</cite> surveyed the way in which realism and personalization enhance attack success, nonetheless offering a limited synthesis of the landscape of phishing attacks and defense. Despite the substantial body of research on phishing detection and classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">101</a>]</cite>, few have addressed defense strategies against the recent changes of phishing attacks launched by LLMs. Surveys conducted by Wu et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">96</a>]</cite> and Kumarage et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite> collectively map out the broad landscape of LLM vulnerabilities and countermeasures, yet address phishing generation merely as a peripheral instance of “misuse” rather than a subject of dedicated study.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Interdisciplinary research in LLM and text-based phishing susceptibility has gained increasing attention in recent years and is continuing to expand with proliferated across diverse perspectives, scopes, and focal points, underscoring the need for a systematized taxonomy of LLM-driven phishing landscape and variability. To fill this gap, we conducted a meticulous literature review and compiled 44 studies on the intersection of LLMs with text-based phishing campaigns, encompassing the process of generation, attack patterns, and defense, thereby providing full-stage coverage of the phishing lifecycle. We proposed Generation-Characterization-Defense (GenCharDef), a multi-stage and multi-dimensional comparative framework that i) systematically reveals how LLMs enable phishing generation, how these capabilities manifest in real-world attack tactics, and how current defense mechanisms respond; and ii) identifies open challenges and research opportunities arising from these findings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Ultimately, our <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">contributions</span> are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We contribute to the first end-to-end SoK on LLM-generated scripted phishing campaigns, enabling a holistic understanding of the LLM-phishing threat landscape.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We contribute to the first multi-stage, multi-dimensional comparative framework for examining LLM phishing generation techniques, attack features, and mitigation methods.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We contribute comparative insights that highlight how LLM-generated phishing differs from traditional phishing across methodologies, security perspectives, feature characteristics, data dependencies, and evaluation practices.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We contribute to reviewing challenges of datasets and evaluation metrics used within end-to-end LLM-phishing studies, facilitating the construction of datasets and standardization for future benchmarking.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We contribute to a gap analysis and identification of open challenges across generation, attacks, and detection, outlining directions for future research.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Roadmap</span>. The rest of the paper is organized as follows. We begin with a brief introduction to LLM in Section <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ SoK: Large Language Model-Generated Textual Phishing Campaigns End-to-End Analysis ofGeneration, Characteristics, and Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Methods of Paper Selection ‣ SoK: Large Language Model-Generated Textual Phishing Campaigns End-to-End Analysis ofGeneration, Characteristics, and Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the keywords we used to filter papers, and Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Background ‣ SoK: Large Language Model-Generated Textual Phishing Campaigns End-to-End Analysis ofGeneration, Characteristics, and Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is an introduction to phishing campaigns at the phases of generation, attacks, and detection. Section <a href="#S4" title="4 RQ1. Exploitation Techniques for Phishing Text Generation across Different LLMs ‣ SoK: Large Language Model-Generated Textual Phishing Campaigns End-to-End Analysis ofGeneration, Characteristics, and Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> presents the overview of employing LLMs for phishing content synthesis. Section <a href="#S5" title="5 RQ2. Analysis of Characteristics in Emerging Phishing Attacks Generated by LLMs ‣ SoK: Large Language Model-Generated Textual Phishing Campaigns End-to-End Analysis ofGeneration, Characteristics, and Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> reported studied attack vectors of LLM-driven pretext attacks, which is followed by defense technologies focused on LLM-weaponized content phishing (Section <a href="#S6" title="6 RQ3. Defense and Detection Techniques against LLM-Enabled Textual Phishing Campaigns ‣ SoK: Large Language Model-Generated Textual Phishing Campaigns End-to-End Analysis ofGeneration, Characteristics, and Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). In Section <a href="#S7" title="7 Future Directions ‣ SoK: Large Language Model-Generated Textual Phishing Campaigns End-to-End Analysis ofGeneration, Characteristics, and Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, we discussed the future directions of research in the domain of LLM-driven phishing campaigns. We conclude the work in Section <a href="#S8" title="8 Conclusion ‣ SoK: Large Language Model-Generated Textual Phishing Campaigns End-to-End Analysis ofGeneration, Characteristics, and Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2508.21457v1/GED.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/179;" width="698" height="179" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">A Full-Lifecycle Taxonomy of LLM-Enabled Phishing: From Generation, Characterization to Defense. The taxonomy traces how different LLMs are exploited to generate phishing content with varied techniques (RQ1); how these attacks exhibit distinctive patterns and linguistic features (RQ2); and how tailored detection and defense strategies address LLM-facilitated text-based phishing attacks.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Methods of Paper Selection</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Preliminary Preparation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The target of this survey is to collect current state research about text-based phishing campaigns provided by LLMs, understand differences in phishing patterns after evolving writing methods, and get the hang of shortcomings among proposed defenses against phishing methods. The following research questions are discerned with the above objectives:</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RQ1: How are Large Language Models exploited to generate phishing content, circumventing detection?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RQ2: What are the distinctive characteristics of LLM-generated phishing content?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RQ3: What countermeasures have been proposed to defend against LLM-text-based phishing campaigns?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, RQ1 examines how LLMs are exploited for text-based phishing, including prompts and training methods that bypass safeguards. RQ2 investigates the distinctive characteristics of LLM-generated phishing compared with human-written content, highlighting common generative and persuasive strategies. RQ3 reviews countermeasures against such attacks, exposing current limitations and guiding future defenses. An overview is shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ SoK: Large Language Model-Generated Textual Phishing Campaigns End-to-End Analysis ofGeneration, Characteristics, and Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We designed search strings to conduct our research. Searching scope has been confined to <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“phishing content”</span> first to generally retrieve the main works in this area. Where <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“fake content”</span> is excluded from the query, as it differs from research goals, motivations, and technologies compared to <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“phishing”</span>. Synonyms such as <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“spam”, “malicious”, and “fraudulent”</span> are also considered because they are commonly used in phishing attacks. Additionally, <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“text”, “pretext”</span> are replacement of <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“content”</span>. Then, the available approaches for phishing content creation are largely centered on Large Language Models. Replaced phrases such as <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“AI-driven”, “Generative AI (GAI) generated”, and “synthetic”</span> were included during searching to ascertain that related work was not omitted. Note that the abbreviation <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLMs</span> was excluded, given that distinct phrases might converge into the same expression. As a result of permutations and combinations, we produced 48 groups of search strings. For example, <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“synthetic phishing text”, “AI-driven phishing content”</span> were iteratively used for the initial screening of the articles.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Execute the Searching</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Resources of paper scoping from <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ACM Digital Library, IEEE Xplore, Springer Link, and Google Scholar</span>, collected papers published on the top conferences (<span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">IEEE S&amp;P, ACM CCS, USENIX Security, NDSS, CHI, ACSAC, PETs, and RAID</span>) and journals (<span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">JCS, Computer &amp;Security, Journal of Information Security and Applications</span>). In light of practical considerations, we also included discussions on articles published as preprints on <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ArXiv</span>. The selection cutoff for the articles was set at the emergence of the first LLMs in 2018. The representative older papers for comparison were also included. These searches give rise to 3,910 references after duplicate works are removed (Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.2 Execute the Searching ‣ 2 Methods of Paper Selection ‣ SoK: Large Language Model-Generated Textual Phishing Campaigns End-to-End Analysis ofGeneration, Characteristics, and Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
Then, screening papers based on corresponding RQs. Showing RQ1 as an example, the majority of unrelated work concerns LLMs in malicious activities or explores the future prospects of LLMs in specific domains. We finally conducted a reading of 44 papers, including the proposed methodology, results, and discussion. The list of core papers is available in the spreadsheet at GitHub online<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://anonymous.4open.science/r/ssc_LLM_phishing_survey-BF08/README.md" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://anonymous.4open.science/r/ssc_LLM_phishing_survey-BF08/README.md</a></span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2508.21457v1/paper_selection.svg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:698/928;" width="698" height="928"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Flow diagram summarizing paper screening along the three research questions.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The screened papers are classified based on research questions and distributed in Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.2 Execute the Searching ‣ 2 Methods of Paper Selection ‣ SoK: Large Language Model-Generated Textual Phishing Campaigns End-to-End Analysis ofGeneration, Characteristics, and Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> during the period of 2018 to June 2025. Some works address multiple aspects simultaneously and are therefore counted across different RQs, making the number of papers actually discussed larger than the category totals.
In recent years, particularly in 2025, several relevant studies have appeared first on arXiv; these were also considered in our review to ensure breadth and timeliness, though they may later be formally published. Overall, there has been a growing trend across all three RQs after 2023. In 2024 alone, 24 papers were accessible, accounting for 54.5% of the total collected works, and further growth is expected with more diverse attack methods, defense solutions, and comparative analyses likely to emerge in future research.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2508.21457v1/paper_distribution.svg" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:628/521;" width="628" height="521"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S2.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The distribution of screened paper that covers the work for each year based on research questions (from 2018 to 2025.6). Numbers inside the bars denote the count of studies focused on generation, characterization, and defense. The numbers in front of each bar are the total count of papers in that year.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Overview of Text-based Phishing campaigns</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Phishing is a prevalent form of social engineering that deceives victims into revealing information or unintentionally installing malware on their devices through counterfeit email, websites, or SMS that is designed to motivate unconscious actions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">95</a>]</cite>. As the IBM 2024 phishing report <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> shows that at least 80% of cyber breaches start with phishing and cost 4.88 million per breach on average. Additionally, the Verizon 2024 Data Breach Investigations Report <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> illustrates that victims’ reaction times of under one minute for malicious link clicks and only 28 seconds for private data submission. These reports underscore the urgency and effectiveness of users being attacked, framing text-based vectors as a primary medium for exploitation in diverse scenarios. Thus, in our work, the attack payload is set as text (<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g., email, SMS</span>) as it may expand to diverse domains, such as text-to-image/speech/video. Webpage phishing content and JavaScript-created text are temporarily excluded from this work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Large Language Models in Phishing Generation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years, phishing campaigns skyrocketed after the introduction of ChatGPT in late 2022, contributing to a 4151% increase <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">88</a>]</cite>. Phishers have employed the advent of LLMs successfully, leading phishing creation into a more automated process. The generation capability of LLMs in context-aware and stylistically adaptive generation makes phishing content generation exhibit flexibility in personalization, style imitation, and multilingual attack reach, which reduces the effectiveness of detection methods relying on surface-level linguistic errors or fixed templates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">99</a>]</cite>. Worse, empirical studies have shown that LLM-generated phishing scripts achieve higher click-through and deception rates comparable to those of human-authored scripts in certain controlled experiments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>. In parallel, security vendors and intelligence agencies have disclosed cases in which organized threat actors leveraged LLM to support operations, including crafting more deceptive phishing emails <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, underscoring the urgency of defending against model-driven phishing.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Model-generated Phishing Detection</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">According to the features that hackers crafted in bulk phishing, human experts were trying to label phishing through function keywords such as “urgent,” “immediately,” “alert notification,” and the like. Traditional phishing detection mechanisms leverage ML methods modeling patterns of content structure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>; or DL methods incorporating relational content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>. While these approaches adapt to phishing campaigns for formulated phishing with obvious attack patterns, the rapid changes of LLM-generated content impose substantial challenges to the generalization capacity of such detection methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Thus, whether content signs of machine generation increase the research focus, where typical approaches include statistical watermarking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>, probability and curvature features (DetectGPT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite>, and visualization-based statistical forensics (GLTR) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>. However, phishing detection presents domain-specific challenges that generic LLM-text detection approaches do not fully address, as LLM-generated phishing content tends to be more natural, adaptable, and persuasive than traditional attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>RQ1. Exploitation Techniques for Phishing Text Generation across Different LLMs</h2>

