</span></div></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_figure_panel">
<li id="S4.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:505.9pt;">
<p id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Paradigms</span><span id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> indicate the design logic of phishing generation methods, distinguishing attacks that are driven by data manipulation, external knowledge integration, crafted prompting strategies, or adversarial training objectives.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:505.9pt;">
<p id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Attacks</span><span id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> refer to modifications of different stages in the LLM generation process, categorized as data-level</span><span id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:70%;"> (D)</span><span id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">, prompt-level </span><span id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:70%;">(P)</span><span id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">, and model-level </span><span id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:70%;">(M)</span><span id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul></div></div>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Datasets Usage and Resources</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Model training resources often derive from continuously updated corpora such as the Nazario Corpus and Nigerian Fraudulent dataset, enabling researchers to acquire the latest deceptive strategies employed by phishers. Some studies have changed toward private datasets (e.g., Bethany et al.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>) or aggregated multi-source data (e.g., Chen et al.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>) to enhance training diversity (summaries of datasets are available in Appendix Table <a href="#Ax4.T5" title="Table A5 ‣ Tables with Bibliographic Numbers ‣ SoK: Large Language Model-Generated Textual Phishing Campaigns End-to-End Analysis ofGeneration, Characteristics, and Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A5</span></a>). Changes also include non-traditional sources such as fake news, spam reviews, and prompt repositories, suggesting that LLM-generated phishing is expanding beyond email into multimodal and cross-domain settings. Overall, current research indicates that dataset construction for LLM-driven phishing remains exploratory, with a growing need for semantically rich and context-aware resources to match the generation capacity and attack diversity of modern models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Phishing Content Generation with LLMs</h3>

<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.1 </span>Data-Guided Phishing Generation</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A. Task Adapted Model Fine-Tuning</span> retrains a pre-trained language model on domain-specific datasets, adapting the model parameters for phishing text generation. Guo et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> leveraged a training pair <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(topic, keywords)</span> that was extracted from both phishing (i.e., Fraud Email) and legitimized (i.e., News Category) emails to fine-tune GPT-2 and BART. The trained models generate targeted phishing scripts given by specific topic and keyword pairs. Mehdi et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite> fine-tuned GPT-2 with phishing observations on the dataset of IWSPA 2.0 to mass-generate phishing emails. The study, conducted by Guo et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, introduced language adapters pre-trained on phishing datasets in specific languages to enhance the stylistic and linguistic diversity. Panda et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite> studied to elicit memorized sensitive information (initially masked by a placeholder) from a trained model. The model specifically remembers a field that injects a set of benign trigger sentences with masked secrets that appear once in context (e.g., “Employee ID: [Secret]”). By querying the model with similar triggers at inference, the model auto-regressively completes the sequence to reveal the masked secrets for malicious intent. Wani et al.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">92</a>]</cite> trained ChatGPT to mimic user-generated review styles by prompting the model with the datasets of reviews with personality features.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B. Knowledge Augmented Generation</span> refers to methods that, at the inference stage, incorporate externally retrieved knowledge (e.g., names, personal profiles), into the model’s context to ground and guide generation beyond what is contained in the static training datasets. Hazell <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> leveraged GPT-4 to write a Python script for each UK MP’s Wikipedia page, then fed the raw page text to GPT-3.5 to produce a structured biography (e.g., name, position, works, awards, etc.) for generating personalized phishing emails at scale. Perik et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>]</cite> replaced random names in LLM-generated scammed collusion with realistic pseudonyms from the <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Faker</span> library <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>. Bi et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> introduced AR glasses to capture and distill audio, and environmental cues (e.g., facial expressions, acoustic energy, scene objects), while a role-based multimodal RAG stack retrieves and fuses target-specific public social data (e.g., Instagram&amp;LinkedIn posts, historical interactions) to the model to adaptively generate persuasive dialogue to direct social engineering interactions in time.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.2 </span>Prompt-Guided Phishing Generation</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A. Human-Crafted Prompts</span>
<span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:10.4pt;position:relative; bottom:-2.0pt;"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="100.00" height="100.00" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,100.00) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(0,0)"><circle fill="none" r="0" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4" x="0" y="0"/></g><g transform="translate(0,0)"><g transform="scale(1,-1)"><text xml:id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.pic1.1">1</text></g></g></g></svg></span></span><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> Basic Instruction Prompting</span> denotes conditioning a pre-trained LLMs via a plain-text directive, optionally accompanied by task inputs and light constraints, without external retrieval tools, or stepwise reasoning scaffolds. Task-oriented prompt design provides a direct means to steer generation in phishing studies. Eze et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> used DeepAI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">93</a>]</cite> to issue explicit malicious prompts, such as generating a bank transfer phishing email. Afane et al.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> prompted the model to directly rewrite phishing emails, while Utaliyeva et al.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>]</cite> introduced more structured guidance by specifying synonym substitution and sentence restructuring in the prompt. Xue et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">97</a>]</cite> synthesized phishing scripts by character substitution using homoglyphs and polymorphic variation to evade detection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond general rewriting, several studies adopt scenario-specific prompting to simulate targeted phishing campaigns. Emabuela et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> instructed ChatGPT to impersonate official entities such as police departments in coercive phishing scenarios. Karanjai et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> adopted a prompt that was customized to impersonate a university president requesting students to complete a course survey using a call-to-action (e.g., survey links). Fredrik et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> targeted students with GPT-4 prompts exploiting the Starbucks brand. Different from the scene variants, Francia et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> incorporated demographics (e.g., age, gender) for crafting smishing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Prompts or customized with crafted rules of generation. Chen et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> proposed a keyword-driven, multi-stage prompting framework that fuses instruction directives (e.g., “you are an expert in cybersecurity”), label conditioning (e.g., “phishing”), topical keyword constraints, and template instantiation. The pipeline enables the model to produce structured content aligned with attack intent. Langford <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite> asked the model to generate phishing emails using given brands (e.g., PayPal, Twitter) and malicious links. Fredrik et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> leveraged the V-Triad behavioral framework (comprising credibility, compatibility, and customizability) into customized prompt templates, tailoring content to align with user routines and capabilities, enhancing the credibility of synthesized phishing scripts. Ekekihl et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> constrained 150 wording of ChatGPT provided phishing samples. Mahendru et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite> exploited GPT-4 to generate phishing and benign emails along with classification explanations to support the realism of phishing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Prompts encode social engineering tactics toward targeted, persuasive phishing content generation. Studies conducted by Bethany et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, Carelli et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, and Greco et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> instructed to tie phishing content that emphasized Cialdini principles, which were referenced in placeholder <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“[principle]”</span>, enabling the model to autonomously select and apply specific strategies during generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:10.4pt;position:relative; bottom:-2.0pt;"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.pic1" class="ltx_picture"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="100.00" height="100.00" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,100.00) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(0,0)"><circle fill="none" r="0" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4" x="0" y="0"/></g><g transform="translate(0,0)"><g transform="scale(1,-1)"><text xml:id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.pic1.1">2</text></g></g></g></svg></span></span><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> Conditional Prompting</span> refers to a prompt design strategy in which the generation process is guided by explicit or implicit logical conditions, such as <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if-then</span> statements, allowing the model to selectively compose part of the output. Kang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> structures the prompts as a pseudo-program, where payload components (e.g., email header, body) are defined as separated strings and concatenated only at runtime through instructions that are executed only when predefined conditions are met (e.g., “if <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in b</annotation></semantics></math>, then return <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=a+b+c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=a+b+c</annotation></semantics></math>”).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:10.4pt;position:relative; bottom:-2.0pt;"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.pic1" class="ltx_picture"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="100.00" height="100.00" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,100.00) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(0,0)"><circle fill="none" r="0" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4" x="0" y="0"/></g><g transform="translate(0,0)"><g transform="scale(1,-1)"><text xml:id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.pic1.1">3</text></g></g></g></svg></span></span><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> Sequential Prompting</span> leverages sequential prompt design and conversational context to indirectly circumvent safety mechanisms without issuing overtly malicious instructions. Singh et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">87</a>]</cite> designed a multi-turn prompting approach that gradually steers the model toward malicious output. Starting with vague or exploratory statements, the conversation avoids explicit intent, then strategically injects increasingly targeted questions framed in neutral or hypothetical terms (e.g., script generation, profit inquiry, or data manipulation scenarios). Patel et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite> and Shibli et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>]</cite> leveraged Sequential prompt chaining within realistic email reply scenarios to circumvent the privacy-preserving behavior of LLMs, where Patel et al. leveraged sequential prompt chaining within realistic email reply scenarios to invert the privacy-preserving logic of LLMs. Early turns referenced neutral content such as a comfortable flight or meeting summary, while later prompts introduced requests for sensitive information, including payment details. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B. Model-Crafted Prompting</span>
Model bootstrap prompts refer to an automated method in which LLMs are seeded with initial prompts (either handcrafted by humans or derived from generation objectives) and subsequently tasked with generating new prompt variants. Roy et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite> and Young et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">100</a>]</cite> proposed an automated workflow for generating phishing prompts using GPT-4. Starting with a manually crafted seed prompt, the model is prompted to replicate and diversify prompt variants. These generated prompt sets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> are then individually fed back into the LLM to elicit phishing content. This feedback loop enables the model to autonomously expand the attack surface by generating novel malicious prompts that guide further phishing content generation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.3 </span>Adversarially-Guided Phishing Generation</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Phishing content is provided by iteratively optimizing through adversarial interaction with a simulated or actual phishing defender. Khan et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> framed phishing generation as an attacker-defender game, with GPT-2 as the attacker and a phishing email classifier simulating the defender. By optimizing generation strategies, they maximized attacker payoff and minimized cost, producing more phishing emails with a higher score. Fairbanks et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> proposed a generation-evaluation loop, where the generator produced candidate emails with default parameter settings and beam search, while a separate evaluator model scored the outputs against a quality threshold and provided feedback for refinement to improve the overall attack quality.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Learn from the work</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Conditional and sequential prompting turns the contextual conversation into a channel for safety bypass. Unlike one-shot text generation, LLM conversation memory and context accumulation create a sequence-level attack surface. The multi-turn dialog steers start from a neutral context and incrementally introduce more specific, sensitive queries, challenging privacy protections in actual reply threats. The risk arises not from a malicious instruction, but from the progressive revelation of intent and the cross-turn persistence of state. The line of the work implies that evaluation and defense should model and constrain the interaction process, not rely solely on static, single-sample checks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, the study conducted by Panda et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite> indicates the susceptibility of LLMs to trigger-induced memorization leakage, whereby a one-shot injected secret and corresponding placeholder trigger sentence elicits long-tail memory at inference, resulting in reconstruction and exfiltration of masked fields. This stems from large-scale pretraining and the features of the auto-regression mechanism of LLMs’ structure, which have rarely been reproduced by non-LLMs. Thus, assessments of LLM-generated phishing should go beyond static phishing indicators to probe memory triggerability triggering conditions, and the length of potential leakage chains, and fold these into red teaming and benchmark design.</p>
</div>
