</span></div></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_figure_panel">
<li id="S4.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:505.9pt;">
<p id="S4.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Objects</span><span id="S4.I2.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> refer to the specific focus of analysis, such as contextual features of text, social engineering attributes, user demographics, or model traits.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:505.9pt;">
<p id="S4.I2.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Attack Vector Focus</span><span id="S4.I2.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> denotes whether the study investigates vulnerabilities at the model, text, or user interaction level.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:505.9pt;">
<p id="S4.I2.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Analytical Approaches</span><span id="S4.I2.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> categories to i) </span><span id="S4.I2.ix3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Experimental Evaluation</span><span id="S4.I2.ix3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> rely on controlled experiments or real-world data to evaluate attributes of LLM-generated phishing; ii) </span><span id="S4.I2.ix3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">User-Centric Analysis</span><span id="S4.I2.ix3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> assesses user perception and behavior through case studies and psycholinguistic evaluations; and iii) </span><span id="S4.I2.ix3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Conceptual Analysis</span><span id="S4.I2.ix3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> focuses on theoretical reasoning, feature extraction, or framework design to interpret phishing text properties.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In evaluating LLM-generated phishing, existing studies approach the problem from several textual and behavioral perspectives. The most common angles include fluency and readability (e.g., BLEU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite>, ROUGE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>, perplexity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>), credibility and persuasiveness (via human judgment or expert review), and resistance to detection (classifier-based signals). Some studies indirectly assess effectiveness through linguistic diversity, lexical variation, or the use of persuasion principles such as urgency or authority <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>. Beyond text quality, a few works also consider attack success rates in controlled user studies, reflecting whether generated messages can elicit intended actions. Overall, current evaluation emphasizes surface-level text quality and basic deception cues, while more holistic measures of real-world effectiveness, user susceptibility, and adaptive evasion remain underexplored.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>RQ2. Analysis of Characteristics in Emerging Phishing Attacks Generated by LLMs</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Datasets Usage and Resources</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For comparative studies of LLM-generated versus human-written phishing texts, a limited set of datasets has been repeatedly adopted. Among human-written corpora, the most widely used are the Nazario phishing collection and the Nigerian fraud dataset, both serving as traditional baselines. In addition, user-written samples created during controlled studies appear frequently, covering both phishing and legitimate emails. On the synthetic side, most LLM-generated datasets are private and tied to specific research targets, with only a few disclosed publicly, while a subset falls under <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">N.A.</span>, where LLMs are employed for text transformation rather than direct release. Overall, Nazario, Nigerian fraud, and user-written corpora dominate the comparative analysis, while synthetic datasets remain fragmented and largely unavailable, as summarized in Table <a href="#Ax4.T5" title="Table A5 ‣ Tables with Bibliographic Numbers ‣ SoK: Large Language Model-Generated Textual Phishing Campaigns End-to-End Analysis ofGeneration, Characteristics, and Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.1 </span>Textual Traits</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A. Textual Characteristics</span> Eze and Lior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> analyzed 11 textual attributes (e.g., pronoun frequency, verb frequency, part-of-speech diversity) to compare AI-synthesized and human-written phishing content, finding statistically significant differences in word usage. AI-generated phishing emails contained more verbs and pronouns but fewer cardinal numbers than human-written messages. Fredrik et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> highlighted that subtle linguistic and formatting cues, such as grammatical or spelling mistakes, can affect perceived credibility and increase the likelihood of compliance. In related work, they showed that AI surpassed human experts in crafting contextually tailored justifications for taking action (e.g., clicking malicious links), producing more concrete and recipient-specific messages.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Bouchareb and Morad <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> observed that AI-generated phishing emails were more likely to reach recipients’ inboxes due to plain text formatting, clear subject lines, and the avoidance of overt phishing markers. Carelli <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> examined 30 text features, including average word length, POS tag frequency, and lexical diversity, visualizing how each feature contributed to distinguishing AI- from human-written phishing emails. Afane et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> found that LLM-based rewriting of phishing emails induced statistically significant lexical shifts toward more benign language, altering classifier decision boundaries and reducing detection accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B. Social Engineering Tactics</span>
Chen et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> proposed the PEEK framework for generating high-quality, diverse phishing emails and analyzing attack patterns. The study found that AI-generated phishing content more frequently relied on the persuasive principles of <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Scarcity</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Authority</span>, and <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consistency</span>, while human-authored phishing emails commonly employed <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Authority</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reciprocity</span>, and <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Scarcity</span>. In addition, the authors observed that LLM-generated pretext often exhibit grater <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cognitive complexity</span> that evident from integrated multiple manipulation psychological triggers to increase user engagement and reduce the ability to critical assess legitimacy of given content. Emanuela et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> conducted a user study on AI-generated social engineering attacks. While 72% of participants were able to identify of finance-related (e.g., tax income) phishing content framed with <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">scarcity</span>, the correct classification rate dropped to 37% when <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">authority</span> was combined with <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">scarcity</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.2 </span>Human Factors</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A. Individual Characteristics</span> Alahmed et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> examined how user traits such as professional background, IT literacy, and social media habits relate to vulnerability in AI-generated social engineering attacks. They found that users with lower IT literacy, including students and non-technical workers, were more likely to fall for phishing content produced by large language models. In contrast, users with technical expertise were more prone to trust deepfake voice attacks that mimicked authority figures.
Francia et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> examined how user demographics (i.e., age, occupation, and self-reported technical ability) affect responses to AI- and human-generated spear phishing SMS messages. While these traits showed no significant impact on susceptibility, messages aligned with participants’ occupations led to higher click rates, with job-related content reaching 38%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B. Psychological Characteristics</span> Bharati <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> highlighted cognitive overload as a key vulnerability exploited by AI, where individuals’ decision-making process become impaired under excessive information stimuli. Sharma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>]</cite> reported that statistical variations in cognitive bias, indicating that individuals with higher levels of curiosity and trust are more likely to comply with phishing requests. Asfour <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> found that individuals high in agreeableness are more susceptible to AI-generated phishing, as their greater inclination to trust message content increases compliance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.3 </span>Model Traits</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Humans often struggle to detect phishing attacks, a challenge amplified by the recent advances in LLM capabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>. The evolution of AI-driven text generation has significantly reduced the cost and increased the scale of phishing campaigns. Schmitt and Flechais <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>]</cite> assessed the time, cost, and effort required for attackers to use LLMs in phishing. The study found that phishing campaigns powered by LLMs could generate targeted content 96% faster than human operators and increase recipient engagement rates by 214%, indicating a tripling of effective threat potential at a fraction of traditional effort.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Learn from the work</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLM-generated phishing content shows a stable evolution in language and presentation. Prior work reports higher usage of verbs and pronouns and less reliance on numerals, with measurable POS-profile differences relative to human phishing. Attack methods of rewriting amplify the attack effect by pulling the pretext toward legitimate distributions and avoiding explicit phishing indicators. The persuasion cues become denser, which makes changes to decision boundaries and hinders the performance of distinguishing accuracy. Prior works have studied each factor in isolation, yet their interactions within a unified pipeline remain underexplored. Future work can, in contextualized scenarios, examine how LLMs use strategically implicit guidance to induce users to execute malicious actions.</p>
</div>
