</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:505.9pt;">
<p id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Detection Level</span><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">: The semantic granularity of detection, Lexical level (</span><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:70%;">L</span><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">), Semantic level (</span><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:70%;">S</span><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">), Tactic level (</span><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:70%;">T</span><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">), Intention level (</span><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:70%;">I</span><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">).</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In studies analyzing exploitation patterns of LLM-generated phishing texts, four main methodological strands are observed. Quantitative metrics were used in 4 studies, focusing on execution time, computational cost, and resource consumption through custom formulations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>. Qualitative evaluation appeared in 5 studies, assessing linguistic and grammatical features with case-based user studies or expert checks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>]</cite>. Analytical summaries were employed in 5 works, mainly using descriptive statistics and visualization to present attribute distributions and trends <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. Finally, statistical testing was applied in 3 studies, where methods such as ANOVA, Chi-square, and Mann-Whitney U were used to identify significant differences across groups <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>. A detailed distribution of these evaluation methods is summarized in Appendix Table <a href="#Ax4.T4" title="Table A4 ‣ Tables with Bibliographic Numbers ‣ SoK: Large Language Model-Generated Textual Phishing Campaigns End-to-End Analysis ofGeneration, Characteristics, and Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>RQ3. Defense and Detection Techniques against LLM-Enabled Textual Phishing Campaigns</h2>

<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Datasets Usage and Resources</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The review of recent studies shows that approximately 60% of the datasets used for detecting LLM-generated phishing content rely on private or non-reproducible synthetic samples, and publicly available resources remain limited. Only a small number of works utilize open-source corpora such as the Prompt Code Book <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite>. Given the evolving characteristics of LLMs, including frequent version changes and variability in output, identical prompts may produce different phishing samples across time. This introduces inconsistency in dataset construction and poses challenges for reproducibility. In addition, around 40% of the studies do not clearly report the sources or preprocessing methods for legitimate email data, further limiting comparability across experiments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Synthetic Phishing Detection</h3>

<section id="S6.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.2.1 </span>Machine Assistant Phishing Detection</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A. Prompt-Screening</span>
Roy et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite> framed malicious prompt detection as a supervised classification task. They compiled a dataset of 3,364 model instructions consisting of phishing-inducing and benign instructions, including email-generation prompts that explicitly attempt to elicit phishing emails from LLMs. A RoBERTa-base model fine-tuned on this dataset achieved 94% accuracy in identifying phishing-related email prompts and maintained strong performance across prompts targeting different LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B. Content-Screening</span>
<span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:10.4pt;position:relative; bottom:-2.0pt;"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="100.00" height="100.00" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,100.00) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(0,0)"><circle fill="none" r="0" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4" x="0" y="0"/></g><g transform="translate(0,0)"><g transform="scale(1,-1)"><text xml:id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.pic1.1">1</text></g></g></g></svg></span></span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> Text &amp; Structure Patterns Screening</span> Elongha and Liu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> detected GPT-4o-generated phishing emails using a stacking ensemble of Random Forest (RF) and Convolution Neural Networks (CNN), with a neural network meta-learner to learn the distribution of predictions from RF and CNN. Trained on healthcare-adapted datasets generated by GPT-4o, the meta learner achieved near-perfect accuracy, while the authors acknowledged potential overfitting due to the limited diversity of the training data. Wani et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">92</a>]</cite> detect AI-generated spam reviews via deep neural models (CNN, LSTM, BiLSTM) combined with embeddings (TF-IDF, Word2Vec). The best model, BiLSTM with Word2Vec, reached 98.46% accuracy. Gryka et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> examined AI-generated email detection using Random Forest trained on lexical and structural features such as spelling errors, punctuation patterns, sentence length, and grammar mistakes. The trained model achieved F1 scores of 0.99 at English phishing datasets, 0.92 on Polish datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Carelli <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> extracted 30 textual features from Nazario phishing data involving structural context features (e.g., average length of the sentence), semantic and lexical features (e.g., bigram, trigram, etc.), and coherence features (e.g., coherence) to train classifiers. A neural network classifier using lexical richness, syntax depth, and sentiment cues reached 99.78% accuracy and 99.99% AUC. Similarly, Greco et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> trained multiple ML classifiers from the perspective of lexical and structural indicators, such as punctuation frequency, lexical diversity from Nazario and Nigerian Fraud datasets involving 11 attack topics. The results indicate Logistic Regression model outperforms other ML models. Eze et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> explored the method of Machine Learning for Language Toolkit (MALLET) for topic modeling and Universal Data Analysis (UDAT) aimed at contextual features (e.g., art of speech, repetition of words) to represent phishing indicators that are contained in Nigerian Fraud phishing datasets and generic human-written data. The result reveals Naive Bayes model trained on phishing patterns extracted by UDAT performs the best.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:10.4pt;position:relative; bottom:-2.0pt;"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="100.00" height="100.00" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,100.00) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(0,0)"><circle fill="none" r="0" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4" x="0" y="0"/></g><g transform="translate(0,0)"><g transform="scale(1,-1)"><text xml:id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p4.pic1.1">2</text></g></g></g></svg></span></span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> Semantic &amp; Intention Screening</span>
Bethany et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> conducted an experiment to distinguish LLM-generated phishing emails from human-written ones using semantic representations. Specifically, they employed a detection system based on frozen T5 encoder embeddings to capture high-level semantic features of the email content. These embeddings were passed to a multilayer perceptron (MLP) classifier, trained on a balanced domain-specific dataset comprising 5,000 human-written emails and LLM-generated counterparts (from Vicuna-13B). After fine-tuning, the model achieved an F1-score of 0.98, demonstrating high discriminative capacity for identifying stylistic and semantic differences between human and machine-generated text.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Xue et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">97</a>]</cite> proposed a multi-agent phishing guard that was dedicated text agent models that semantic information from phishing emails using LLM embedding. By encoding sentence and paragraph-level representations, the safeguard captures semantic coherence and intent. These semantics vectors are fused with URL and metadata, and the weights are dynamically optimized by reinforcement learning (PPO). Each agent follows a crafted prompt to output a confidence score and a verdict. An explanation agent translates semantic cues (e.g., suspicious intent, context mismatches) into human-readable justifications, achieving 97.89% accuracy overall. Perik <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>]</cite> examined semantic detection in social engineering scams on YouTube by leveraging transformer-based embeddings. Scam and non-scam texts were transformed into contextual representations capturing intent, topical alignment, and sentiment polarity. Comparative experiments showed that ML models trained based on transformer embeddings outperformed models relying on TF-IDF and bag-of-words, achieving high accuracy in scam detection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:10.4pt;position:relative; bottom:-2.0pt;"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p6.pic1" class="ltx_picture"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="100.00" height="100.00" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,100.00) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(0,0)"><circle fill="none" r="0" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4" x="0" y="0"/></g><g transform="translate(0,0)"><g transform="scale(1,-1)"><text xml:id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p6.pic1.1">3</text></g></g></g></svg></span></span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> Policy-Compliance Screening</span> Quinn and Thompson <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>]</cite> introduced a protocol generation approach for phishing defense, instructing the Gemini model with task-specific security records and principles to generate detection strategies. The principles are sourced from email transaction records, spear phishing incident data, and Government, Risk, and Compliance policies, which are iteratively refined to adapt to evolving threats. Differently, Mahendru and Pandit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite> embedded explicit rules into a prompt to conduct stepwise compliance phishing detections. The rule involves verifying sender authenticity, URLS, evaluating HTML content, and flagging suspicious attachments.
Heiding et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> evaluated pre-trained models such as GPT-4, Claude, PaLM, Llama-2, and Claude 3.5 Sonnet in detecting phishing intent within spear phishing datasets containing both human and LLM-generated samples. The results indicate effectiveness in intent classification, though model performance varied and was sometimes sensitive to prompt phrasing.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.2.2 </span>Human-Centric Defense</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Malloy et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> focused phishing detection on identifying individual weaknesses to improve resilience against phishing. The authors framed phishing defense as improving user detection skills through cognitive modeling. Building on recorded user decisions, confidence, and actions across human- and GPT-4-generated phishing conditions, the authors proposed an Instance-Based Learning (IBL) model that encodes emails with GPT-4 embeddings to predict user reactions over time, and dynamically selects training examples to strengthen their detection ability.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2508.21457v1/attack_defense.svg" id="S6.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:628/463;" width="628" height="463"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S6.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Full-lifecycle taxonomy of LLM-generated text-based phishing campaigns, spanning generation techniques, emerging attack characteristics, and corresponding detection and defense strategies. Lines on the right refer to a defense technique that can counter a specific attack or a group of attacks. Lines on the left represent studied text-based features arising from different generation methods.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span>Learn from the work</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mechanisms for detecting LLM-generated phishing texts increasingly emphasize intent-level analysis and input-stage screening, rather than relying solely on lexical or structural indicators. Recent studies incorporate prompt screening and semantic embeddings, often enhanced through multi-agent fusion of content features, URLs, and metadata. Despite these advances, intent classifiers remain highly sensitive to phrasing and display inconsistent performance across models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>. Moreover, while content screening frequently achieves strong results on single-source or synthetic datasets, its effectiveness declines substantially under cross-generator, cross-domain, and cross-language transfer conditions (Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2 Learn from the work ‣ 5 RQ2. Analysis of Characteristics in Emerging Phishing Attacks Generated by LLMs ‣ SoK: Large Language Model-Generated Textual Phishing Campaigns End-to-End Analysis ofGeneration, Characteristics, and Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.4 </span>Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In current studies on detecting LLM-generated phishing content, accuracy remains the most frequently reported metric (13 studies), followed by precision, recall, and F1-score (8 studies). These measures capture overall classification performance but are largely inherited from traditional binary detection tasks and provide limited insight into challenges such as the subtlety and variability of LLM-generated phishing. A smaller number of works employed confusion matrices (3 studies) and AUROC (2 studies), offering more fine-grained perspectives. In addition, a few studies introduced evasion-oriented measures such as EVA-ACC and EVA-F1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">92</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> to evaluate robustness under adversarial conditions. Nevertheless, around 60 percent of the studies still rely primarily on standard classification metrics, with limited attention to the dynamic and high-fidelity characteristics of LLM-generated phishing. This suggests that current evaluation practices remain rooted in conventional approaches, while dedicated metrics tailored to emerging generative threats are still lacking. A detailed breakdown of detection evaluation metrics is provided in Appendix Table <a href="#Ax4.T4" title="Table A4 ‣ Tables with Bibliographic Numbers ‣ SoK: Large Language Model-Generated Textual Phishing Campaigns End-to-End Analysis ofGeneration, Characteristics, and Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Future Directions</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S7.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adaptive Sequential Contextual Phishing Modeling</span>. This survey examines two main approaches to phishing text generation with LLMs, prompt-based elicitation and supervised learning on phishing data. We find the majority of existing methods remain static and single-turn, falling short of reflecting the dynamic nature of realistic attacks. While some work explores multi-turn and task-chained generation, it lacks modeling of evolving attackers’ intent, strategy adaptation, and contextual coherence. Studies on sequential phishing generation increase interaction rounds without capturing semantic continuity or goal transitions, and feedback-driven approaches rely on artificial settings with limited user data. Future research could model how attacker goals evolve over time through planning-based generation that adjusts tactics across stages <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">98</a>, <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Understanding Human-AI Interaction Pathways in Phishing Susceptibility.</span> Based on the review of recent works on LLM-generated phishing text attacks, we find that the majority of work on phishing attack success is associated with psychological indicators such as urgency and authority. Recent studies increasingly place trust in AI-driven systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>, while trust remains an underexplored cognitive dimension. Future work can explore how cognitive indicators, such as trust in AI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite>, influence user susceptibility and decision-making in AI-mediated contexts. Furthermore, personalized phishing simulation frameworks may be advanced by mining user profiles from interaction history, involving expressive features, personality traits, and behavioral patterns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">86</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. In the context of unsupervised learning, exploring the phishing adaptability and tracing the evolution of deception strategies, while approximating user decision-making in response to attacks, contributes to the development of human-centered, intelligent defense systems.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Constructing and Evaluating LLM-Based Phishing Corpora.</span> Although the an advancement in defending against LLM-driven text-based phishing attacks, proposed detectors are often trained on outdated and limited corpora that fail to capture the evolving nature of phishing attacks. Compared to traditional datasets, LLM-generated samples can exhibit greater fluency, contextual alignment, and adversarial strength, especially when targeting known detector weaknesses. Future research could leverage the generative capacity to construct a dynamic and diverse phishing dataset centered on LLM outputs, covering varied strategies, styles, and target profiles. This requires clear multidimensional quality metrics (e.g., covering realism, manipulation strength, contextual relevance, and evasiveness) to guide generation and sample selection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. The dataset can fuel adversarial training pipelines that fine-tune detectors and close the loop between generation and defense, improving the resilience of phishing detection systems.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Addressing Stealth and Reliability under LLM Phishing Generation.</span> Current detection methods for LLM-generated phishing have moved beyond binary classifiers to analyzers, incorporating rule-based analysis and policy-guided evaluation. However, the analyzers are constrained by stealthy phishing attacks, where emails are crafted to appear indistinguishable from benign communications. Additionally, intention-detection systems often label emails as phishing without adequately accounting for false positives, which leads to frequent over-flagging of legitimate messages.
Future research could adapt techniques from anomaly detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">83</a>]</cite> and representation-level consistency checks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite> to uncover implicit phishing intent in ordinary-looking texts while ensuring evaluation frameworks balance accuracy and false positive control <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we have presented the first systematization of knowledge on LLM-generated phishing, covering the attack cycle from generation to mitigation. To our knowledge, this is the first comprehensive survey that examines LLM-enabled phishing in a structured and end-to-end manner, and the only work that develops a multi-stage, multi-dimensional taxonomy to highlight its distinctive characteristics and challenges. Our review identifies several gaps and opportunities for future research. In particular, current work has largely focused on static or single-turn generation, leaving intent migration and contextual coherence underexplored. Dataset construction and evaluation metrics remain limited, hindering rigorous benchmarking of phishing defenses. Detection research has not yet adequately addressed stealthy phishing content or incorporated robust evaluation of false positives. By consolidating fragmented findings and outlining these gaps, our systematization provides a coherent foundation that can guide the design of more resilient detection and defense strategies against evolving phishing threats.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Ethical Considerations</h2>

<div id="Ax1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work is a systematization of knowledge (SoK) on LLM-generated phishing. Our analysis relies on prior published research and, where datasets are used, only on those that are publicly available. We do not redistribute any raw datasets; instead, we provide citations to their original sources and follow their licensing conditions. The inclusion of these datasets serves only for academic analysis and benchmarking. No new phishing content was collected from real users, and no executable attack pipelines are released. The purpose of this work is defensive: to clarify the threat landscape and support the design of more resilient detection and defense mechanisms. We believe the study complies with the USENIX Security ethics guidelines by minimizing risks of misuse while maximizing benefits for the security community.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Open Science</h2>

<div id="Ax2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In accordance with the USENIX Security open-science policy, we commit to releasing all non-sensitive artifacts generated by this SoK. These include: (1) our multi-stage, multi-dimensional taxonomy and classification framework, (2) structured tables and figures summarizing prior work, and (3) our annotated bibliography of surveyed research. For datasets, we rely exclusively on corpora that are already public; these will not be redistributed by us. Instead, we provide references and metadata to guide readers to their original sources. This ensures reproducibility while respecting licensing and ethical constraints. All artifacts under our control will be made available in anonymized form at submission and released publicly upon acceptance.</p>
</div>
</section>
